00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.600 align:start position:0%
european union finance ministers have  given a green light to the european  commission to negotiate toughest savings  tax agreements with non-eu countries  Switzerland Liechtenstein Monaco and  Dora and San Marino the decision is part  of EU moves to clamp down on tax evasion  and tax fraud at their meeting in  Brussels The ministers also pledged to  promote good tax practices and tax  governance in countries outside the EU  high on the agenda were plans to  strengthen rules for taxes on savings  income the plan is to make it possible  to tax all kinds of savings income and  products that generate interest as well  as make it harder to get around the  rules currently in place we also had  good discussions today on the savings  tax directive not all Member States were  able to agree to these proposals a day  we expect that this issue will be on the  agenda of the European Council next week  The ministers also agreed to amend the  EU budget for this year with an  additional 7.3 billion euros moves  toward an EU banking Union were on the  table to the ministers discussed new  rules aimed at pre-empting bank crises  and dealing with problems like  insolvency when they happen the measures  would establish the order in which  investors would be obliged to help bail  out an institution in trouble there was  broad agreement that depositors was less  than 100,000 euros in savings would not  be called on to contribute more broadly  the rules would require banks and  investment firms to draw up recovery  plans laying out the measures they would  take to restore their financial health  if things turn sour
 

